WEBVTT

00:00:15.283 --> 00:00:18.090
DAVID SONTAG: OK, so
then today's lecture

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.290
is going to be about data set
shift, specifically how one can

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.240
be robust to data set shift.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.220
Now this is a topic that
we've been alluding to

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.845
throughout the semester,
and the setting

00:00:31.845 --> 00:00:33.970
that I want you to be
thinking about is as follows.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.180
You're a data scientist
working at, let's say,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.440
Mass General Hospital.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.990
And you've been very careful
in setting up your machine

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.100
learning task to make sure that
the data is well specified.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.480
The labels that you're trying
to predict are well specified.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.960
You train on your training data.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.810
You test it on a held out set.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.688
You see that the model
generalizes well.

00:01:00.688 --> 00:01:02.230
You do chart review
to make sure what

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.647
you're predicting
is actually what

00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:05.560
you think you're predicting.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.350
And you even do prospective
deployment, where you then

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.315
let your machine
learning algorithm drive

00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:11.940
some clinical decision
support, and you

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.940
see things are working great.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.986
Now what?

00:01:16.986 --> 00:01:21.690
What happens after this stage,
when you go to deployment?

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.110
What happens when
your same model

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.060
is going to be used
not just tomorrow

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.370
but also next week, the
following week, the next year?

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.640
What happens if your model,
which is working well

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.180
at this one hospital--

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.910
then there's
another institution,

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:41.460
say maybe Brigham Women's
Hospital or maybe UCSF

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.530
or some rural hospital
in the United States

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.660
wants to use the same model.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.820
Will it keep working
in this short term

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.675
to the future time period
or in a new institution?

00:01:50.675 --> 00:01:52.800
That's the question which
we're going to be talking

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.080
about in today's lecture.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.770
And we'll be talking
about how one

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.910
could deal with data set shift
of two different varieties.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:03.630
The first variety is adversarial
perturbations to data.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.270
The second variety
is the data that

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.300
changes for natural reasons.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.490
Now the reason why
it's not at all obvious

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.590
that your machine learning
algorithm should still

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.470
work in this setting is because
the number one assumption

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.330
we make when we do
machine learning

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.250
is that you're
training distribution.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.950
You're training data is drawn
from the same distribution

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.583
as your test data.

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:27.000
So if you now go to a setting
where your data distribution

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.210
has changed, even if you've
computed your accuracy using

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:35.797
your holdout data
and it looks good,

00:02:35.797 --> 00:02:37.380
there's no reason
that should continue

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.620
to look good in this new setting
where the data distribution has

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.630
changed.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.325
A simple example of what it
means for data distribution

00:02:45.325 --> 00:02:46.575
to change might be as follows.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:57.740
Suppose that we
have as input data

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:05.760
and we're trying to predict
some label which maybe meant

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:13.470
something like y is if a patient
has or will be newly diagnosed

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.930
with type 2 diabetes.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:20.680
And this is an example which we
talked about when we introduced

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.200
risk stratification.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.580
You learn a model
to predict y from x.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.060
And now, suppose you
go to a new institution

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.600
where their definition of
what type 2 diabetes means

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.750
has changed.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:42.170
For example, maybe they don't
actually have type 2 diabetes

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.070
coded in their data.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.160
Maybe they only
have diabetes coded

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.390
in their data, which is lumping
together both type 1 and type 2

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:56.330
diabetes-- type 1 being what's
usually a juvenile diabetes

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.830
and is actually a very distinct
disease from type 2 diabetes.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.430
So now the notion of what
diabetes is different.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.505
Maybe the use case is
also slightly different.

00:04:04.505 --> 00:04:05.880
And there's no
reason, obviously,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.490
that your model which was used
to predict type 2 diabetes

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.930
would work for that new label.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.810
Now this is an example
of a type of data

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.958
set shift which is, perhaps
for you, kind of obvious.

00:04:17.958 --> 00:04:19.750
Nothing should work in
this setting, right?

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:26.395
Because here, the distribution
of p of y given x changes.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.890
Meaning even if you have
the same individual,

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:36.540
your distribution py given x--
and let's say the distribution

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.230
p of 0 and the
distribution p of y

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.213
given x in p 1, where
this is one institution,

00:04:42.213 --> 00:04:43.380
this is another institution.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.150
These now are two
different distributions

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.160
if the meaning of the
label has changed.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.730
So for the same person, there
might be different distribution

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:52.870
over what y is.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:54.515
So this is one type
of data set shift.

00:04:54.515 --> 00:04:55.890
And a very different
type of data

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.303
set shift is where we assume
that these two are equal.

00:04:59.303 --> 00:05:00.970
And so that would,
for example, rule out

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.170
this type of data set shift.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:12.360
But rather what changes is p of
x from location 1 to location

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.390
2, OK?

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.980
And this is the type of data set
shift which will be focused on

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:18.840
in today's lecture.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.225
It goes by the name
of covariant shift.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.240
And let's look at two
different examples of that.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.840
The first example would be of
an adversarial perturbation.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:39.670
And so you've all seen the
use of convolutional networks

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.530
for image
classification problems.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:44.470
This is just one illustration
of such an architecture.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:45.970
And with such an
architecture, one

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.220
could then attempt to do all
sorts of different object

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.980
classification or image
classification tasks.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:54.250
You could take as input
this picture of a dog which

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:58.080
is clearly a dog, right?

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.230
And you could modify
it just a little bit.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:05.238
Just add in a very
small amount of noise.

00:06:05.238 --> 00:06:06.780
What I'm going to
do is I'm now going

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.950
to create a new image which
is that original image.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.680
Now with every single
pixel, I'm going

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.940
to add a very small epsilon in
the direction of that noise.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.700
And what you get out
is this new image,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.250
which you could stare at
it however long you want.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:24.330
You're not going to be
able to tell a difference.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.400
Basically, to the
human eye, these two

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.330
look exactly identical.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.660
Except when you take your
machine learning classifier,

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.080
which is trained on
original unperturbed data,

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.340
and now apply it
to this new image

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.590
it's classified as an ostrich.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.350
And this observation
was published

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:50.300
in a paper in 2014 called
Intriguing Properties of Neural

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.310
Networks.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:58.255
And it really kickstarted
a huge surge of interest

00:06:58.255 --> 00:06:59.630
in the machine
learning community

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:04.350
on adversarial perturbations
to machine learning.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.730
So asking questions, if you
were to perturb inputs just

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.210
a little bit, how does that
change your classifiers output?

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:15.140
And could that be used to attack
machine learning algorithms?

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.122
And how can one
defend against it?

00:07:18.122 --> 00:07:19.830
By the way, as an
aside, this is actually

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.590
a very old area of
research and even back

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.085
in the land of
linear classifiers,

00:07:26.085 --> 00:07:27.460
these questions
had been studied.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.930
Although I won't get
into it in this course.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:32.960
So this is a type of data
set shift in the sense

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:36.910
that what we want is that this
should still be classified

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.240
as as a dog, right?

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.200
So the actual label
hasn't changed.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.410
We would like this
distribution over the labels

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.960
given the perturbed into it
to be slightly different.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.690
Except that now, that sort
of distribution of inputs

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:51.640
is a little bit
different because we're

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.507
allowing for some noise to be
added to each of the inputs.

00:07:55.507 --> 00:07:57.590
And in this case, the noise
actually isn't random,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.310
it's adversarial.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.935
And towards the end
of today's lecture,

00:07:59.935 --> 00:08:02.540
I'll give an example
of how one can actually

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:06.730
generate the adversarial image,
which can change the crossfire.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.770
Now the reason
why we should care

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.530
about these types of
things in this course

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:15.310
are because I expect that
this type of data set shift--

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:18.040
which is not at all
natural, it's adversarial--

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.240
is also going to start showing
up in both computer vision

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:26.570
and non computer vision
problems in the medical domain.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:30.620
There was a nice paper
by Sam Philipson,

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:35.270
Andy Beam, and
Zack Cohen recently

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:37.039
which presented
several different case

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:40.370
studies of where these
problems could really

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.620
arise in healthcare.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:44.450
So for example, here
what we're looking at

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:45.980
is an image
classification problem

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.770
arising from dermatology.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:52.170
You're given as input an image.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:57.540
For example, you would like
that this image be classified

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:01.500
as an individual having
a particular type of skin

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.430
disorder nevus, and this
other image melanoma.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.270
And what one can see is that
with a small perturbation

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:13.720
of the input, one
can completely swap

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.050
the label that would be assigned
to it from one to the other.

00:09:19.502 --> 00:09:20.960
And in this paper
which we're going

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.320
to post as optional
readings for today's course,

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.650
they talk about how one
could maliciously use

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.220
these algorithms for benefit.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:36.890
So for example, imagine that
a health insurance company now

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:44.440
decides in order to reimburse
for a expensive biopsy

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:50.600
of a patient's skin,
a clinician or nurse

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:56.090
must first take a
picture of the disorder

00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:00.500
and submit that picture
together with the bill

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:02.510
for the procedure.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.710
And imagine now that
the insurance company

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.070
were to have a machine
learning algorithm be

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.440
an automatic sort of check.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:15.240
Was this procedure actually
reasonable for this condition?

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.830
And if it isn't, it
might be flagged.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:25.640
Now a malicious user could
perturb the input such

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:27.980
that it would, despite
the patient having

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:31.520
perhaps even completely
normal looking skin,

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.940
nonetheless be classified by
a machine learning algorithm

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.280
as being abnormal in
some way, and thus

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.170
perhaps could get reimbursed
by that procedure.

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:46.780
Now obviously this is an example
of a nefarious setting, where

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:51.220
we would then hope that such
a individual would be caught

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:53.793
by the police, sent to jail.

00:10:53.793 --> 00:10:55.960
But nonetheless, what we
would like to be able to do

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.617
is build checks and balances
into the system such

00:10:58.617 --> 00:11:00.200
that that couldn't
even happen, right?

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.740
Because to a human,
it's kind of obvious

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:06.970
that you shouldn't be able
to trick anyone with such

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:09.022
a very minor perturbation.

00:11:09.022 --> 00:11:10.480
So how do you build
algorithms that

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.640
could also be not
tricked as easily

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:15.434
as humans wouldn't be tricked?

00:11:15.434 --> 00:11:17.360
AUDIENCE: For any
of these examples,

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.000
did the attacker need
access to the network?

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.012
And is there a way to
do it if they didn't?

00:11:22.012 --> 00:11:24.470
DAVID SONTAG: So the question
is whether the attacker needs

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.960
to know something about
the function that's

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.450
being used for classifying.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:33.170
There are examples of both what
are called white box and black

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:37.430
box attacks where
in one setting,

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:43.500
you have access to the function,
and other settings you don't.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.670
So both have been studied
in the literature,

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.460
and there are results
showing that one

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:49.620
can attack in either setting.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.780
Sometimes you might need
to know a little bit more.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.280
Like for example,
sometimes you need

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.980
to have the ability to query
the function a certain number

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:56.718
of times.

00:11:56.718 --> 00:11:59.010
So even if you don't know
exactly what the function is,

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.560
if you don't know the weights
of the neural network,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.710
as long as you can query
it sufficiently many times

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.620
you'll be able to construct
adversarial examples.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:08.880
That would be one approach.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:10.770
Another approach would be maybe
we don't know the function,

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:12.720
but we know something
about the training data.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:16.070
So there are ways to go about
doing this even if you don't

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:17.510
perfectly know the function.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:18.802
Does that answer your question?

00:12:21.504 --> 00:12:24.997
So what about a
natural perturbation?

00:12:24.997 --> 00:12:26.830
So this figure is just
pulled from lecture 5

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:28.372
when we talked about
non stationarity

00:12:28.372 --> 00:12:30.520
in the context of
risk stratification.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:33.380
Just to remind you,
here the x-axis is time.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:37.000
The y-axis is different types
of laboratory test results

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:38.410
that might be ordered.

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:43.540
And the color denotes how
many of those laboratory

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.690
tests were ordered in a certain
population at a point in time.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:50.980
So what we would expect to see
if the data is was stationary

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:54.040
is that every row would
be a homogeneous color.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.540
But instead what we see is
that there are points in time--

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:58.840
for example, a few months
interval over here--

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:02.890
when suddenly it looks like, for
some of the laboratory tests,

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.860
they were never performed.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:08.590
That's most likely
due to a data problem

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:11.830
or perhaps the feed of data from
that laboratory test provider

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:12.710
got lost.

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:14.470
There were some
systems problems.

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:16.387
But there are also going
to be settings where,

00:13:16.387 --> 00:13:18.160
for example, a
laboratory test is never

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:19.660
used until it's suddenly used.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.100
And that may be because
it's a new test that

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.170
was just invented or
approved for reimbursement

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:24.620
at that point in time.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.410
So this is an example
of non stationarity,

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.980
and of course this could also
result in changes in your data

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.460
distribution, such as what
I described over there,

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.341
over time.

00:13:35.341 --> 00:13:37.080
And the third example
is when you then

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:40.470
go across institutions, when
of course both the language

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:41.380
that might be used--

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:43.460
think of a hospital
in the United States

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:45.030
versus a hospital in China.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.447
The clinical notes will be
written in completely different

00:13:47.447 --> 00:13:48.230
languages.

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:49.920
That would be an extreme case.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.100
And a less extreme case might
be two different hospitals

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:56.155
in Boston where the
acronyms, or the shorthand

00:13:56.155 --> 00:13:58.140
that they use for
some clinical terms

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.120
might actually be different
because of local practices.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.530
So what do we do?

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:05.400
This is all a setup.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.830
And for the rest of the
lecture, what I'll talk about

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:10.800
is first, very
briefly, how one can

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:15.190
build in population level checks
for has something changed.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.980
And then the bulk
of today's lecture

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.610
we'll be talking about how
to develop transfer learning

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:23.837
algorithms and how one
could think about defenses

00:14:23.837 --> 00:14:24.795
to adversarial attacks.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.770
So before I show you that
first slide for bullet one,

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.477
I want to have a
bit of discussion.

00:14:38.807 --> 00:14:41.390
You've suddenly done that thing
of learning a machine learning

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:44.240
algorithm in your
institution, and you

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:50.900
want to know will this algorithm
work at some other institution.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:52.130
You pick up the phone.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.280
You call up your
collaborating data scientist

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:56.367
at that other institution.

00:14:56.367 --> 00:14:58.700
What are the questions that
you should ask them in order

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.242
to try and understand will your
algorithm work there as well?

00:15:07.845 --> 00:15:08.345
Yeah?

00:15:08.345 --> 00:15:10.620
AUDIENCE: What kind of
lab test information

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:13.390
they collect regularly.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.960
DAVID SONTAG: So
what type of data

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.120
do they have on their
patients, and do they

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:20.420
have similar data types
or features available

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:22.880
for their patient population?

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:25.130
Other ideas, someone who
hasn't spoken in the last two

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.560
lectures.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.252
Maybe someone in
the far back there,

00:15:29.252 --> 00:15:30.710
people who have
their computer out.

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:32.835
Maybe you with your hand
in your mouth right there.

00:15:32.835 --> 00:15:34.580
Yeah, you with the glasses on.

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:35.866
Ideas?

00:15:35.866 --> 00:15:37.640
AUDIENCE: Can you
repeat the question?

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.420
DAVID SONTAG: You want me
to repeat the question?

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:42.760
The question was as follows.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:45.855
You learn your machine learning
algorithm at some institution,

00:15:45.855 --> 00:15:48.682
and you want to apply it
now in a new institution.

00:15:48.682 --> 00:15:50.890
What questions should you
ask of that new institution

00:15:50.890 --> 00:15:53.182
to try to assess whether your
algorithm will generalize

00:15:53.182 --> 00:15:54.761
to that new institution?

00:15:54.761 --> 00:15:58.410
AUDIENCE: I guess it depends on
your problem you're looking at.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:00.977
Are there possible differences
in your population?

00:16:00.977 --> 00:16:03.040
If you're acquiring data
with particular tools,

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:06.710
what are the differences in
the tools that are being used?

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.680
Are their machines
calibrated differently?

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.955
Do they use different
techniques to acquire the data?

00:16:10.955 --> 00:16:12.330
DAVID SONTAG: All
right, so let's

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.760
break down each of the
answers that you gave.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.423
The first answer
that you gave was

00:16:16.423 --> 00:16:18.090
are there differences
in the population.

00:16:21.288 --> 00:16:23.580
Someone else now, what would
an example of a difference

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:24.709
in a population?

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:29.380
Yep.

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:31.797
AUDIENCE: Age distribution,
where they have younger people

00:16:31.797 --> 00:16:34.020
in Boston versus central
Massachusetts, for example.

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:35.853
DAVID SONTAG: So you
may have younger people

00:16:35.853 --> 00:16:40.730
in Boston versus older people
over in central Massachusetts.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.890
How might a change
in age distribution

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:47.150
affect your ability of your
algorithm to generalize?

00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.790
AUDIENCE: It's possible that
health patterns for younger

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:52.103
people are very different
than that for older people.

00:16:52.103 --> 00:16:53.520
Perhaps some of
the diseases there

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.080
are more prevalent in
populations that are older.

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.080
DAVID SONTAG: Thank you.

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:01.040
So sometimes we might expect
a different set of diseases

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:03.680
to occur for a younger
population versus older

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.609
population, right?

00:17:04.609 --> 00:17:07.970
So type 2 diabetes,
hypertension-- these

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:13.640
are diseases that are often
diagnosed when individuals

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:17.540
are 40s, 50s, and older.

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:19.280
If you have people
who are in their 20s,

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:21.290
you don't typically
see those diseases

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.310
in a younger population.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:27.440
And so what that means is
if your model, for example,

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:32.750
was trained on a population
of very young individuals,

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:36.260
then it might not be able to--

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.960
suppose you're doing something
like predicting future cost,

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.870
or so something which
is not directly tied

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:43.550
to the disease itself.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.370
The features that are predictive
of future cost in a very

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:50.680
young population might be
very different from features

00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.880
for predictors of cost in
a much older population,

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.310
because of the
differences in conditions

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.930
that those individuals have.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.580
Now the second
answer that was given

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.640
had to do with calibration
of instruments.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.536
Can you elaborate
a bit about that?

00:18:03.536 --> 00:18:04.240
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:09.060
So I was thinking related
in the colonoscopy space.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.960
So if, in that space, you're
collecting videos of colons,

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.120
and so you could have
machines that are

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:15.320
calibrated very differently.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:18.150
Let's say different light
exposure, different camera

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:18.650
settings.

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:21.662
But you also have the
GIs, and the physicians

00:18:21.662 --> 00:18:24.120
have different techniques as
to how they explore the colon.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.730
So the video data itself is
going to be very different.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:28.580
DAVID SONTAG: So the
example that was given

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.230
was of colonoscopies
and data that might

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:32.650
be collected as part of that.

00:18:35.590 --> 00:18:37.760
And the data that
could be collected

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:39.730
could be different for
two different reasons.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:44.018
One, because the
actual instruments

00:18:44.018 --> 00:18:46.310
that are collecting the data,
for example imaging data,

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:47.690
might be calibrated a
little bit differently.

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:49.940
And second reason might be
because the procedures that

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:53.180
are used to perform that
diagnostic test might be

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:54.740
different in each institution.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:57.780
Each one will result in slightly
different biases to the data,

00:18:57.780 --> 00:18:59.930
and it's not clear that
an algorithm trained

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:02.210
on one type of procedure
or one type of instrument

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.620
will generalize to another.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:06.680
So these are all great examples.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:11.720
So when one reads a paper
from the clinical community

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:16.580
on developing a new risk
stratification tool, what

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:19.940
you will always
see in this paper

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:23.060
is what's known as table one.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:25.400
Table one looks a
little bit like this.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.530
Here, I've pulled
one of my own papers

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:29.955
that was published in
JAMA Cardiology for 2016,

00:19:29.955 --> 00:19:32.330
where we looked at how to try
to find patients with heart

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:34.970
failure who are hospitalized.

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:37.560
And I'm just going to walk
through what this table is.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.040
So this table is
describing the population

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.840
that was used in the study.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:46.130
At the very top, it says these
are characteristics of 47,000

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:48.030
hospitalized patients.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:53.540
Then what we've done is,
using our domain knowledge,

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:55.870
we know that this is a
heart failure population.

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.240
And we know that there are
a number of different axes

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.390
that differentiate patients
who are hospitalized

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:02.950
that have heart failure.

00:20:02.950 --> 00:20:07.150
And so we enumerate over
many of the features

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:10.840
that we think are critical to
characterizing the population,

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.880
and we give
descriptive statistics

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.740
on each one of those features.

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:19.920
You always start with things
like age, gender, and race.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.990
So here, for example, the
average age was 61 years old.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:29.080
This was, by the way,
NYU Medical School.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:34.870
50.8% female, 11.2%
black African-American.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:38.930
17.6% of individuals
were on Medicaid,

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:41.290
which was the state
provided health

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:47.060
insurance for either disabled
or lower income individuals.

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:51.330
And then we looked at quantities
like what types of medications

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:54.740
were patients on.

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:58.560
42% of inpatient patients
were on something

00:20:58.560 --> 00:20:59.850
called beta blockers.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:04.050
31.6% of outpatients
were on beta blockers.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:09.130
We then looked at things
like laboratory test results.

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:12.390
So one could look at the
average creatinine values,

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:17.010
the average sodium values
of the patient population,

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.500
and this way describe what
is the population that's

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:21.473
being studied.

00:21:21.473 --> 00:21:23.140
Then when you go to
the new institution,

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:26.440
that new institution receives
not just the algorithm,

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:29.092
but they also receive
this table one

00:21:29.092 --> 00:21:30.550
that describes the
population which

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:32.980
the algorithm was learned on.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:36.040
And they could use that together
with some domain knowledge

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:39.430
to think through questions
like what I elicited

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:41.110
from you in our discussion.

00:21:41.110 --> 00:21:43.150
So we could think,
does it make sense

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:45.490
that this model will generalize
to this new institution?

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:48.010
Are the reasons
why it might not?

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:49.840
And you could do that
even before doing

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:54.790
any prospective evaluation
on the new population.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:57.450
So almost all of you
should have something

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:02.030
like table one in your
project write ups,

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:05.900
because that's an important
part of any study in this field,

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:07.940
describing what is the
population that you're

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:09.460
doing your study on.

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:10.616
You agree with me, Pete?

00:22:10.616 --> 00:22:14.110
AUDIENCE: I would just add
that table one, if you're

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:19.210
doing a case control study,
will have two columns that

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:24.680
show the distributions
in both populations,

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:28.780
and then a p value of how
likely those differences are

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:30.196
to be significant.

00:22:30.196 --> 00:22:33.750
And if you leave that out, you
can't get your paper published.

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:35.720
DAVID SONTAG: I'll just
repeat Pete's answer

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:36.560
for the recording.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:44.280
This table is for a
predictive problem.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:47.360
But if you're thinking about
a causal inference type

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:50.750
problem where there is a notion
of different intervention

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:54.410
groups, then you'd
be expected to report

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:56.327
the same sorts of things
but for both the case

00:22:56.327 --> 00:22:58.202
population, the people
who received treatment

00:22:58.202 --> 00:22:59.960
one, and the control
population, people

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:02.087
who received treatment zero.

00:23:02.087 --> 00:23:03.920
And then you would be
looking at differences

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.890
between those populations as
well, at the individual feature

00:23:06.890 --> 00:23:09.530
level, as part of
the descriptive

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:11.060
statistics for that study.

00:23:17.015 --> 00:23:20.680
AUDIENCE: Just to identify
[INAUDIBLE] between hospitals,

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:24.285
is it sufficient to do
t test on those tables?

00:23:24.285 --> 00:23:26.240
DAVID SONTAG: To see
if they're different.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:27.800
So they're always going
to be different, right?

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.175
You go to a new
institution, it's

00:23:29.175 --> 00:23:31.350
always going to look different.

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:34.590
And so just looking to see
has something changed--

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:37.740
the answer is always
going to be yes.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:42.210
But it enables a conversation,
to think through.

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:44.138
And then you might use
some of the techniques

00:23:44.138 --> 00:23:46.680
that Pete's going to talk about
next week on interpretability

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:47.597
to try and understand.

00:23:47.597 --> 00:23:49.410
What is the model
actually using?

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:51.960
Then you might ask,
oh, OK, the model

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.510
is using this thing which
makes sense in this population

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.543
but might not make sense
in another population.

00:23:56.543 --> 00:23:57.960
And it's these two
things together

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.648
that make the conversation.

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:07.560
Now this question
has really come

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:13.830
to the forefront in recent
years in close connection

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:17.130
to the topic that Pete
discussed last week on fairness

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:18.810
in machine learning.

00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:21.660
You might ask if a classifier
is built in some population,

00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:23.130
is it going to generalize
to another population

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:25.547
if that population that it was
learned on was very biased.

00:24:25.547 --> 00:24:27.605
For example, it might have
been all white people.

00:24:27.605 --> 00:24:28.980
You might ask, is
that classifier

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:30.660
going to work well
in another population

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:33.150
that might perhaps
include people

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:34.860
of different ethnicities.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:41.830
And so that has led to a concept
which was recently published.

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:44.910
This working draft that I'm
showing the abstract from

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:50.500
was just a few weeks ago
called Datasheets for Datasets.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.270
And the goal here
is to standardize

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:56.230
the process of eliciting
the information, what

00:24:56.230 --> 00:25:03.740
is it about the data set that
really played into your model.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:05.440
And so I'm going to
walk you through,

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:07.210
very briefly, just
a couple of elements

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:13.330
of what a example data set for
a data sheet might look like.

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:14.950
This is too small
for you to read,

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:18.260
but I'll blow up one
section in just a second.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:21.830
So this is a data
sheet for a data

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:24.857
set called studying
face recognition

00:25:24.857 --> 00:25:26.190
in an unconstrained environment.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.753
So it's for a computer
vision problem.

00:25:28.753 --> 00:25:30.670
There are going to be
number of questionnaires

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.640
which this paper that I
pointed you to outlines.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:38.320
And you, as the model developer,
go through that questionnaire

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.540
and fill out the answers to it.

00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:44.020
So including things about
motivation for the data set

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:46.970
creation, composition,
and so on.

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:51.490
So in this particular instance,
this data set labeled faces

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.730
in the wild was created to
provide images that study face

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:57.520
recognition in
unconstrained settings,

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:00.550
where image character vision
characteristics such as pose,

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:05.570
illumination, resolution, and
focus cannot be controlled.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:10.210
So it's intended to be
real world settings.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.980
Now one of the most
interesting sections

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:16.780
of this report that one should
release with the data set

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:20.110
has to do with how was the
data pre processed or cleaned.

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:21.560
So for example
for this data set,

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:23.500
it walks through the
following process.

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:26.470
First, raw images were
obtained from the data set,

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:31.810
and it consisted of
images and captions that

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:35.190
were found together with
that image in news articles

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.210
or around the web.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:42.150
Then there was a face detector
that was run on the data set.

00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:46.610
Here were the parameters of the
face detector that were used.

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:50.300
And then remember, the goal
here is to study face detection.

00:26:50.300 --> 00:27:00.710
And so one has to know how
were the labels determined.

00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:03.080
And how would one, for
example, eliminate if there

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.160
was no face in this image.

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:09.230
And so there, they describe
how a face was detected and how

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:11.960
a region was determined to not
be a face in the case that it

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:12.890
wasn't.

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:16.568
And finally, it describes
how duplicates were removed.

00:27:16.568 --> 00:27:18.110
And if you think
back to the examples

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:22.340
we had earlier in the
semester from medical imaging,

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:25.400
for example, and
pathology and radiology,

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:28.678
similar data set constructions
had to be done there.

00:27:28.678 --> 00:27:30.470
For example, one would
go to the PAC system

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:33.590
where radiology
images are stored.

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:37.910
One would decide which images
are going to be pulled out.

00:27:37.910 --> 00:27:39.830
One would go to
radiography reports

00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:42.950
to figure out how do we
extract the relevant findings

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:46.910
from that image, which
would give the labels

00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:48.500
for that learning task.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.510
And each step there
will incur some bias,

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:55.550
which one needs to
describe carefully in order

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:57.650
to understand what
might the bias be

00:27:57.650 --> 00:28:00.130
of the learned classifier.

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:03.610
So I won't go into more
detail on this now,

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.770
but this will also be
one of the suggested

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:07.480
readings for today's course.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:08.320
It's a fast read.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:11.340
I encourage you to go through it
to get some intuition for what

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:13.900
are questions we might want
to be asking about data sets

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:14.590
that we create.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:21.900
And for the rest of
the lecture today, I'm

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:24.540
now going to move on to
some more technical issues.

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.180
We're doing machine
learning now.

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:34.280
The populations
might be different.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:35.420
What do we do about it?

00:28:35.420 --> 00:28:37.160
Can we change the
learning algorithm

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:40.430
in order to hope that your
algorithm might transfer better

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:41.510
to a new institution?

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.360
Or if we get a little bit of
data from that new institution,

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:46.850
could we use that
small amount of data

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:50.390
from the new institution
or a point in the future

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:53.990
to retrain our model to
do well in that slightly

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.130
different distribution?

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:59.000
So that's the whole field
of transfer learning.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.870
So you have data drawn from one
distribution, on p of x and y,

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:04.940
and maybe we have a
little bit of data

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:08.630
drawn from a different
distribution, q of x, y.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:11.360
And under the covariance
shift assumption,

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:20.090
I'm assuming that q x
comma y is equal to q

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:25.010
of x times p of
y given x, namely

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:27.500
that the conditional
distribution of y given x

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:28.257
hasn't changed.

00:29:28.257 --> 00:29:29.840
The only thing that
might have changed

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.700
is your distribution over x.

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:35.570
So that's what the covariant
shift assumption would assume.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:42.810
So suppose that we
have some small amount

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:46.820
of data drawn from the
new distribution q.

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:48.690
How could we then
use that in order

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:52.770
to perhaps retrain our
classifier to do well

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:55.390
for that new institution?

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:59.150
So I'll walk through four
different approaches to do so.

00:29:59.150 --> 00:30:02.030
I'll start with
linear models, which

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.010
are the simplest to understand.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:09.810
And then I'll move
on to deep models.

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:12.290
The first approach is something
that you've seen already

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.920
several times in this course.

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:19.260
We're going to think about
transfer as a multi task

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:22.530
learning problem,
where one of the tasks

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:26.002
has much less data
than the other task.

00:30:26.002 --> 00:30:27.960
So if you remember when
we talked about disease

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:31.740
progression modeling,
I introduced

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.980
this notion of
regularizing the weight

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:37.937
vectors so that they could
be close to one another.

00:30:37.937 --> 00:30:40.020
At that time, we were
talking about weight vectors

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:42.020
predicting disease
progression at different time

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:43.050
points in the future.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:46.680
We can use exactly
the same idea here

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:51.630
where you take your linear
classifier that was trained

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:54.960
on a really large corpus.

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:57.570
I'm going to call the weights
of that classifier W old.

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:01.980
And then I'm going to solve a
new optimization problem, which

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:08.010
is minimizing over the weight
w that minimizes some loss.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:11.520
So this is where your new
training data come in.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:26.800
So I'm going to assume that
the new training data D is

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.110
drawn from the q distribution.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:38.430
And now I'm going to add on a
regularization that asks that W

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:40.890
should stay close to W old.

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:50.180
Now if D, the data from
that new institution,

00:31:50.180 --> 00:31:53.150
was very large then you
wouldn't need this at all.

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:02.660
You would be able to ignore
the cross fire that you learned

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:04.580
previously and just
refit everything

00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:06.420
to that new institution's data.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:08.930
Where something like this
is particularly valuable

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:12.380
is if there's a small
amount of data set shift,

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:15.800
and you only have a very
small amount of labeled data

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.420
from that new institution.

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:21.200
Then this would allow you to
change your weight vector just

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:22.180
a little bit, right?

00:32:22.180 --> 00:32:24.320
So if this coefficient
was very large,

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:27.230
it would say that the
new W can't be too far

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:29.490
from the old W.
So it would allow

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:31.730
you to shift things
a little bit in order

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:36.030
to do well on the small
amount of data that you have.

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:39.020
If there is a feature which
was previously predictive

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:41.900
but that feature is no longer
present in the new data set,

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:45.770
so for example it's all
identically 0, then of course

00:32:45.770 --> 00:32:48.872
the new weight for that feature
is going to be set to 0.

00:32:48.872 --> 00:32:50.330
And that weight
you can think about

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:53.873
as being redistributed to
some of the other features.

00:32:53.873 --> 00:32:54.540
This make sense?

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:55.538
Any questions?

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:02.230
So this is the simplest
approach to transfer learning.

00:33:02.230 --> 00:33:04.460
And before you ever try
anything more complicated,

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:05.210
I always try this.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:25.190
So the second approach is
also with a linear model.

00:33:25.190 --> 00:33:28.900
But here, we're no longer going
to assume that the features are

00:33:28.900 --> 00:33:30.790
still useful.

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:39.580
So when you go from
your first institution,

00:33:39.580 --> 00:33:41.878
let's say MGH on the
left, you learn your model

00:33:41.878 --> 00:33:43.920
and you want to apply it
to some new institution,

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:47.170
let's say UCSF on
the right, it could

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:51.520
be that there are some really
big change in the feature set,

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:55.510
such that the original
features are not

00:33:55.510 --> 00:33:59.690
at all useful for
the new feature set.

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:01.570
And a really extreme
example of that

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:03.960
might be the setting
that I gave earlier.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:05.710
You know, your model's
trained on English,

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:08.924
and you're testing it
out on Chinese, right?

00:34:08.924 --> 00:34:10.440
If you used a bag
of words model,

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:14.290
that would be an example
where your model obviously

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:18.639
wouldn't generalize at all
because your features are

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:21.159
completely different.

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:23.287
So what would you
do in that setting?

00:34:23.287 --> 00:34:25.120
What's the simplest
thing that you might do?

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:33.510
So you're taking a text
classifier learned in English,

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:35.100
and you want to
apply it in a setting

00:34:35.100 --> 00:34:36.900
where the language is Chinese.

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:39.719
What would you do?

00:34:39.719 --> 00:34:41.590
Translate, you said.

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:43.747
There was another answer?

00:34:43.747 --> 00:34:45.219
AUDIENCE: Train RNN.

00:34:45.219 --> 00:34:48.969
DAVID SONTAG: Train
an RNN to do what?

00:34:48.969 --> 00:34:53.190
Oh, so assume that
you have some ability

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:55.030
to do machine translation.

00:34:55.030 --> 00:34:57.047
You translate from
Chinese to English.

00:34:57.047 --> 00:34:58.630
It has to be in that
direction because

00:34:58.630 --> 00:35:01.210
your original classifier
was trained in English.

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:04.780
And then your new function
is the composition

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:08.710
of the translation and the
original function, right?

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:11.080
And then you can
imagine doing some fine

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:14.170
tuning if you had a
small amount of data.

00:35:14.170 --> 00:35:19.302
Now the simplest
translation function

00:35:19.302 --> 00:35:21.010
might be just to use
a dictionary, right?

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:23.590
So you look up a
word, and if that word

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:25.690
has an analogy in
another language,

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:27.730
you say OK, this
is the translation.

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:30.230
But there are always going to
be some words in your language

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:33.815
which don't have a
very good translation.

00:35:33.815 --> 00:35:36.190
And so you might imagine that
the simplest approach would

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:38.980
be to translate, but
then to just drop out

00:35:38.980 --> 00:35:43.270
words that don't
have a good analog

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:47.020
and force your classifier
to work with, let's say,

00:35:47.020 --> 00:35:49.732
just the shared vocabulary.

00:35:49.732 --> 00:35:51.190
Everything we're
talking about here

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:54.380
is an example of a
manually chosen decision.

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:56.920
So we're going to manually
choose a new representation

00:35:56.920 --> 00:36:01.730
for the data, such that we
have some amount of shared

00:36:01.730 --> 00:36:05.540
features between the source
and target data sets.

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:11.960
So let's talk about
electronic health record.

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:14.360
By the way, the slides that
I'll be presenting here

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:16.250
are from a paper
published in KDD

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:23.660
by Jan, Tristan, your instructor
Pete, and John Guttag.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:25.520
So you have two
electronic health

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:27.200
records, electronic
health record 1,

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:28.850
electronic health record 2.

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:30.440
How can things change?

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:36.900
Well, it could be that the same
concept in electronic health

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:41.850
record 1 might be mapped
to a different encoding,

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:44.550
so that's like an English
to Spanish type translation,

00:36:44.550 --> 00:36:47.330
in electronic health record 2.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:48.800
Another example
of a change might

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:52.370
be to say that some
concepts are removed.

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:57.040
Like maybe you have
laboratory test results

00:36:57.040 --> 00:36:58.850
in electronic health
record 1 but not

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:00.322
in electronic health record 2.

00:37:00.322 --> 00:37:02.030
So that's why you see
an edge to nowhere.

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:07.935
There might be new concepts.

00:37:07.935 --> 00:37:10.060
So the new institution
might have new types of data

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.170
that the old
institution didn't have.

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:14.170
So what do you do
in that setting?

00:37:14.170 --> 00:37:17.962
Well, one approach,
we could say OK, we

00:37:17.962 --> 00:37:20.170
have some small amount of
data from electronic health

00:37:20.170 --> 00:37:21.400
record 2.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:25.930
We could just train
using that and throw away

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:29.180
your original data from
electronic health record 1.

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:30.930
Of course, if you only
have a small amount

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:33.537
of data from the
target to distribution,

00:37:33.537 --> 00:37:36.120
then that's going to be a very
poor approach because you might

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:37.703
not have enough data
to actually learn

00:37:37.703 --> 00:37:40.020
a reasonable enough model.

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:41.790
A second obvious
approach would be OK,

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:47.370
we're going to just train on
electronic health record 1

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:48.870
and apply it.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:52.860
And for those concepts that
aren't present anymore,

00:37:52.860 --> 00:37:54.360
so be it.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:56.078
Maybe things will
work very well.

00:37:56.078 --> 00:37:58.370
A third approach, which we
were alluding to before when

00:37:58.370 --> 00:37:59.840
we talked about
translation, would

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:02.420
be to learn a model just on
the intersection of the two

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:03.890
features.

00:38:03.890 --> 00:38:06.740
And what this work
does is they say

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:09.320
we're going to manually
redefine the feature set

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:13.125
in order to try to find as
much common ground as possible.

00:38:13.125 --> 00:38:14.750
This is something
which really involves

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:17.510
a lot of domain
knowledge, and I'm

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:20.180
going to be using this
as a point of contrast

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:23.540
from what I'll be talking about
in 10 or 15 minutes, where

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:26.150
I talk about how one could
do this without that domain

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:27.850
knowledge that we're
going to use here.

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:28.350
OK?

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:34.180
So the setting that
we looked at is

00:38:34.180 --> 00:38:37.720
one of predicting outcomes
such as in hospital mortality

00:38:37.720 --> 00:38:40.330
or length of stay.

00:38:40.330 --> 00:38:43.780
The model which is going to be
used is a bag of events model.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:47.770
So we will take a patient's
longitudinal history up

00:38:47.770 --> 00:38:49.390
until the time of prediction.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:52.510
We'll look at different
events that occurred.

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:55.660
And this study was
done using PhysioNet.

00:38:55.660 --> 00:39:01.360
So in Mimic, for example, events
are encoded with some number.

00:39:01.360 --> 00:39:04.870
5814 might correspond
to a CVP alarm.

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:07.620
1046 might correspond
to pain being present.

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:12.580
25 might correspond to the drug
heparin being given, and so on.

00:39:12.580 --> 00:39:16.780
So we're going to create one
feature for every event, which

00:39:16.780 --> 00:39:18.430
is encoded with some number.

00:39:18.430 --> 00:39:21.220
And we'll just say
1 if that event

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:22.850
has occurred, 0 otherwise.

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:27.050
So that's the representation
for a patient.

00:39:27.050 --> 00:39:31.910
Now when one goes to this
new institution, EHR2,

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:36.620
the way that events are encoded
might be completely different.

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.780
One won't be able to just
use the original feature

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:42.020
representation, and that's
the English to Spanish example

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:43.428
that I gave.

00:39:43.428 --> 00:39:44.970
But instead, what
one could try to do

00:39:44.970 --> 00:39:48.780
is come up with a new feature
set where that feature

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:53.190
set could be derived from each
of the different data sets.

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:57.720
So for example since each
one of the events in Mimic

00:39:57.720 --> 00:40:00.480
has some text description
that goes with it--

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:03.390
event 1 corresponds
to ischemic stroke.

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:06.990
Event 2, hemorrhagic
stroke, and so on.

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:12.570
One could use that English
description of the feature

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:15.360
to come up with a way to map
it into a common language.

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:18.360
In this case, the
common language

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:21.000
is the UMLS, the
United Medical Language

00:40:21.000 --> 00:40:23.740
System that Pete talked
about a few lectures ago.

00:40:23.740 --> 00:40:26.730
So we're going to now say, OK,
we have a much larger feature

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:31.650
set where we've now
encoded ischemic stroke

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:34.680
as this concept,
which is actually

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:36.960
the same ischemic
stroke, but also

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:40.740
as this concept and
that concept which

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:43.850
are more general versions
of that original one, right?

00:40:43.850 --> 00:40:46.170
So this is just general stroke.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:49.220
And it could be multiple
different types of strokes.

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:57.410
And the hope is that even if
some of these more specific

00:40:57.410 --> 00:40:59.900
ones don't show up in the
new institutions' data,

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:05.260
perhaps some of the more general
concepts do show up there.

00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:08.770
And then what you're going
to do is learn your model now

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:13.540
on this expanded
translated vocabulary,

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:14.913
and then translate it.

00:41:14.913 --> 00:41:16.330
And at the new
institution, you'll

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:18.520
also be using that
same common data model.

00:41:18.520 --> 00:41:20.800
And that way, one hopes
to have much more overlap

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:23.590
in your feature set.

00:41:23.590 --> 00:41:27.530
And so to evaluate
this, the author

00:41:27.530 --> 00:41:32.290
has looked at two different
time points within Mimic.

00:41:32.290 --> 00:41:36.340
One time points was when the
Beth Israel Deaconess Medical

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:39.290
Center was using electronic
health record called CareVue,

00:41:39.290 --> 00:41:41.645
and the second time point
was when that hospital

00:41:41.645 --> 00:41:43.270
was using a different
electronic health

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:45.710
record called MetaVision.

00:41:45.710 --> 00:41:49.780
This is an example actually
of non stationarity.

00:41:49.780 --> 00:41:54.210
Now because of them using two
different electronic health

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:55.800
records, then codings
were different

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:58.788
and that's why
this problem arose.

00:41:58.788 --> 00:42:00.455
And so we're going
to use this approach,

00:42:00.455 --> 00:42:02.288
and we're going to then
learn a linear model

00:42:02.288 --> 00:42:06.610
on top of this new encoding
that I just described.

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:12.120
And we're going to compare the
results by looking at how much

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:16.890
performance was lost due
to using this new encoding,

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:23.580
and how well we generalize
from the source task

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:26.450
to the target task.

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:28.330
So here's the first
question, which

00:42:28.330 --> 00:42:32.380
is how much do we lose by
using this new encoding?

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:34.210
So as a comparison
point for looking

00:42:34.210 --> 00:42:36.380
at predicting in
hospital mortality,

00:42:36.380 --> 00:42:38.380
we'll look at what is the
predictive performance

00:42:38.380 --> 00:42:42.970
if you're to just use an
existing, very simple risk

00:42:42.970 --> 00:42:45.130
score called the SAP score.

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.220
And that's this red line
where the y-axis here

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:51.520
is the area under the
ROC curve, and the x-axis

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:53.470
is how much time
in advance you're

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:56.150
predicting, so the
prediction gap.

00:42:56.150 --> 00:42:59.540
So using this very
simple score SAP,

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:04.260
get somewhere between 0.75 and
0.80 area under the ROC curve.

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:08.900
But if you were to use all
of the events data, which

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:11.330
is much, much richer than
what went into that simple SAP

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:17.390
score, you would get
the purple curve, which

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:21.300
is SAPS plus the events
data, or the blue curve

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:22.550
which is just the events data.

00:43:22.550 --> 00:43:24.050
And you can see you
can get substantially

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:25.670
better predictive
performance by using

00:43:25.670 --> 00:43:28.540
that much richer feature set.

00:43:28.540 --> 00:43:31.680
The SAP score has the advantage
that it's easier to generalize,

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:33.020
because it's so simple.

00:43:33.020 --> 00:43:35.360
Those feature elements
one can trivially

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:40.610
translate to any new EHR, either
manually or automatically,

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:43.250
and thus it will always
be a viable route.

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:45.220
Whereas this blue
curve, although it

00:43:45.220 --> 00:43:46.830
gets better predictive
performance,

00:43:46.830 --> 00:43:49.460
you have to really worry about
these generalization questions.

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:56.830
The same story happens in both
the source task and the target

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.220
task.

00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:00.970
Now the second question
to ask is, well,

00:44:00.970 --> 00:44:03.640
how much do you lose when you
use the new representation

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:05.660
of the data?

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:13.030
And so here, looking at again
both EHRs, what we see first

00:44:13.030 --> 00:44:17.348
in red is the same
as the blue curve

00:44:17.348 --> 00:44:18.640
I showed in the previous slide.

00:44:18.640 --> 00:44:23.530
It's using SAPS plus the item
IDs, so using all of the data.

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:25.420
And then the blue
curve here, which

00:44:25.420 --> 00:44:27.720
is a bit hard to see
but it's right there,

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:29.050
is substantially lower.

00:44:29.050 --> 00:44:31.360
So that's what
happens if you now

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:33.830
use this new representation.

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:36.340
And you see that you
do lose something

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:40.290
by trying to find a
common vocabulary.

00:44:40.290 --> 00:44:44.870
The performance
does get hit a bit.

00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:46.570
But what's particularly
interesting is

00:44:46.570 --> 00:44:52.520
when you attempt to generalize,
you start to see a swap.

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:59.890
So now the colors are
going to be quite similar.

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:02.840
Red here was at the
very top before,

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:07.510
so red is using the original
representation of the data.

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:10.600
Before, it was at the very top.

00:45:10.600 --> 00:45:15.700
Shown here is the training error
on this institution, CareVue.

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:17.938
You see there's so
much rich information

00:45:17.938 --> 00:45:19.480
in the original
feature set that it's

00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:21.313
able to do very good
predictive performance.

00:45:21.313 --> 00:45:24.370
But once you attempt
to translate it--

00:45:24.370 --> 00:45:28.300
so you train on CareVue,
but you test on MetaVision--

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:32.200
then the test performance shown
here by this solid red line

00:45:32.200 --> 00:45:34.420
is actually the worst
of all of the systems.

00:45:34.420 --> 00:45:36.610
There's a substantial
drop in performance

00:45:36.610 --> 00:45:39.070
because not all
of these features

00:45:39.070 --> 00:45:41.120
are present in the new EHR.

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:44.710
On the other hand, the
translated version--

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:49.540
despite the fact that it's
a little bit worse when

00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:51.250
evaluated on the source--

00:45:51.250 --> 00:45:52.940
it generalizes much better.

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:56.200
And so you see a significantly
better performance

00:45:56.200 --> 00:45:59.290
that's shown by this
blue curve here when you

00:45:59.290 --> 00:46:01.048
use this translated vocabulary.

00:46:01.048 --> 00:46:01.840
There's a question.

00:46:01.840 --> 00:46:05.220
AUDIENCE: So when you
train with more features,

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:10.110
how do you apply the model if
not all the features are there?

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:14.860
DAVID SONTAG: So you assume
that you have come up

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:19.480
with a mapping from the
features in both of the EHRs

00:46:19.480 --> 00:46:24.025
to this common feature
vocabulary of QEs.

00:46:24.025 --> 00:46:26.650
And the way that this mapping is
going to be done in this paper

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:34.540
is based on the
text of the events.

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:36.730
So you take the text based
description of the event,

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:38.563
and you come up with a
deterministic mapping

00:46:38.563 --> 00:46:43.200
to this new UMLS-based
representation.

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:44.705
And then that's
what's being used.

00:46:44.705 --> 00:46:46.080
There's no fine
tuning being done

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:47.205
in this particular example.

00:46:51.180 --> 00:46:56.540
So I consider this to be a very
naive application of transfer.

00:46:56.540 --> 00:46:59.780
The results are exactly what you
would expect the results to be.

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:03.890
And obviously, a lot of work
had to go into doing this.

00:47:03.890 --> 00:47:06.575
And there's a bit of creativity
in thinking that you should use

00:47:06.575 --> 00:47:08.450
the English based
description of the features

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:11.060
to come up with the automatic
mapping, but sort of the story

00:47:11.060 --> 00:47:13.350
ends there.

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:16.490
And so a question
which all of you

00:47:16.490 --> 00:47:18.530
might have is how
could you try to do

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:20.510
such an approach automatically?

00:47:20.510 --> 00:47:23.840
How could we automatically
find new representations

00:47:23.840 --> 00:47:26.550
of the data that are likely
to generalize from, let's say,

00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:29.940
a source distribution to
a target distribution?

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:31.610
And so to talk about
that, we're going

00:47:31.610 --> 00:47:34.580
to now start thinking through
representation learning

00:47:34.580 --> 00:47:37.850
based approaches, of which
deep models are particularly

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:39.910
capable.

00:47:39.910 --> 00:47:43.530
So the simplest approach
to try to transfer

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:47.910
learning in the context of,
let's say, deep neural networks

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.490
would be to just chop
off part of the network

00:47:50.490 --> 00:47:54.820
and reuse some internal
representation of the data

00:47:54.820 --> 00:47:56.860
in this new location.

00:47:56.860 --> 00:48:00.040
So the picture looks a
little bit like this.

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:02.590
So the data might
feed in the bottom.

00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:04.590
There might be a number
of convolutional layers,

00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:05.910
some fully connected layers.

00:48:05.910 --> 00:48:07.368
And what you decide
to do is you're

00:48:07.368 --> 00:48:10.740
going to take this model that's
trained in one institution,

00:48:10.740 --> 00:48:13.260
you chop it at some layer.

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:16.890
It might be, for example, prior
to the last fully connected

00:48:16.890 --> 00:48:18.030
layer.

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:22.140
And then you're going to
take the new representation

00:48:22.140 --> 00:48:23.770
of your data.

00:48:23.770 --> 00:48:25.290
Now the representation
of the data

00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:28.830
is what you would
get out after doing

00:48:28.830 --> 00:48:32.130
some convolutions followed by
a single fully connected layer.

00:48:32.130 --> 00:48:36.150
And then you're going to
take your target distribution

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.840
data, which you might only
have a small amount of,

00:48:38.840 --> 00:48:41.580
and you learn a simple model on
top of that new representation.

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:43.560
So for example, you might
learn a shallow classifier

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.102
using a support
vector machine on top

00:48:45.102 --> 00:48:46.350
of that new representation.

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:51.032
Or you might add in
a couple more layers

00:48:51.032 --> 00:48:53.490
of a deep neural network and
then fine tune the whole thing

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:54.270
end to end.

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:56.440
So all of these have been tried.

00:48:56.440 --> 00:48:59.980
And in some cases, one
works better than another.

00:48:59.980 --> 00:49:05.700
And we saw already one example
of this notion in this course,

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:11.790
and that was when Adam
Yalla spoke in lecture 13

00:49:11.790 --> 00:49:14.520
about breast cancer
and mammography.

00:49:14.520 --> 00:49:17.760
Where in his approach,
he said that he

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:25.130
had tried both taking a
randomly initialized classifier

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:28.040
and comparing that to what
would happen if you initialized

00:49:28.040 --> 00:49:33.080
with a well known image net
based deep neural network

00:49:33.080 --> 00:49:34.460
for the problem.

00:49:34.460 --> 00:49:37.270
And he had a really
interesting story that he gave.

00:49:37.270 --> 00:49:42.170
In his case, he had enough
data that he actually

00:49:42.170 --> 00:49:47.090
didn't need to initialize
using this pre trained model

00:49:47.090 --> 00:49:48.350
from ImageNet.

00:49:48.350 --> 00:49:52.110
If he had just done a random
initialization, eventually--

00:49:52.110 --> 00:49:53.570
and this x-axis,
I can't remember.

00:49:53.570 --> 00:49:57.100
Maybe it might be hours
of training or epochs.

00:49:57.100 --> 00:49:57.830
I don't remember.

00:49:57.830 --> 00:49:58.628
It's time.

00:49:58.628 --> 00:50:00.170
Eventually, the
random initialization

00:50:00.170 --> 00:50:02.180
gets to a very
similar performance.

00:50:02.180 --> 00:50:04.550
But for his particular
case, if you

00:50:04.550 --> 00:50:08.810
were to do a initialization with
ImageNet and then fine tune,

00:50:08.810 --> 00:50:11.000
you get there
much, much quicker.

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:13.070
And so it was for the
computational reason

00:50:13.070 --> 00:50:14.880
that he found it to be useful.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:17.320
But in many other applications
in medical imaging,

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:19.670
these same tricks become
essential because you just

00:50:19.670 --> 00:50:22.030
don't have enough data
in the new test case.

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:25.800
And so one makes use of,
for example, the filters

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:29.150
which one learns from
an ImageNet task which

00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:33.080
is dramatically different from
the medical imaging problems.

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:34.880
And then using those
same filters together

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:38.060
with sort of a new set of
top layers in order to fine

00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:41.285
tune it for the problem
that you care about.

00:50:41.285 --> 00:50:42.660
So this would be
the simplest way

00:50:42.660 --> 00:50:47.100
to try to hope for a common
representation for transfer

00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:49.290
in a deep architecture.

00:50:49.290 --> 00:50:52.530
But you might ask, how would
you do the same sort of thing

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:55.680
with temporal data,
not image data?

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:57.870
Maybe data that's from
a language or data

00:50:57.870 --> 00:51:01.062
from timed series of
health insurance claims.

00:51:01.062 --> 00:51:02.520
And for that, you
really want to be

00:51:02.520 --> 00:51:05.380
thinking about recurrent
neural networks.

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:08.110
So just remind you,
recurrent neural network

00:51:08.110 --> 00:51:10.000
is a recurrent
architecture where

00:51:10.000 --> 00:51:11.822
you take as input some vector.

00:51:11.822 --> 00:51:13.780
For example, if you're
doing language modeling,

00:51:13.780 --> 00:51:16.322
that vector might be encoding
just a one hot encoding of what

00:51:16.322 --> 00:51:17.740
is the word of that location.

00:51:17.740 --> 00:51:20.027
So for example, this
vector might be all zeros

00:51:20.027 --> 00:51:21.610
except for the fourth
dimension, which

00:51:21.610 --> 00:51:25.060
is 1 denoting that this
word is the word "class."

00:51:25.060 --> 00:51:28.810
And then it's fed into
a recurrent unit which

00:51:28.810 --> 00:51:32.105
takes previous hidden
states, combines it

00:51:32.105 --> 00:51:34.480
with the current input, and
gives you a new hidden state.

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:38.080
And in this way, you read in,
you encode the full input.

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:40.490
And then you might predict,
make a classification

00:51:40.490 --> 00:51:42.490
based on the hidden state
of the last timestamp.

00:51:42.490 --> 00:51:44.810
That would be a common approach.

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:47.770
And here would be a very simple
example of a recurrent unit.

00:51:47.770 --> 00:51:49.750
Here I'm using s to
denote the hidden state.

00:51:49.750 --> 00:51:52.487
Often we see h used to
denote the hidden state.

00:51:52.487 --> 00:51:54.820
This is a particularly simple
example where there's just

00:51:54.820 --> 00:51:56.690
a single non linearity.

00:51:56.690 --> 00:51:58.720
So you take your
previous hidden state,

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:03.520
you hit it with some matrix
Wss, and you add that

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:08.800
to the input being hit
by a different matrix.

00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:11.530
You now have a
combination of the input

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:12.833
plus the previous hidden state.

00:52:12.833 --> 00:52:14.500
You apply non linearity
to that, and you

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:15.750
get your new hidden state out.

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:18.930
So that would be an example
of a typical recurrent unit,

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:20.997
a very simple recurrent unit.

00:52:20.997 --> 00:52:23.080
Now the reason why I'm
going through these details

00:52:23.080 --> 00:52:27.610
is to point out that the
dimension of that Wsx matrix

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:32.500
is the dimension of the hidden
state, so the dimension of s,

00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:35.560
by the vocabulary size
if you're using a one

00:52:35.560 --> 00:52:38.120
hot encoding of the input.

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:40.990
So if you have a
huge vocabulary,

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:45.330
that matrix Wss is also
going to be equally large.

00:52:45.330 --> 00:52:47.380
And the challenge
that presents is

00:52:47.380 --> 00:52:52.450
that it would lead to
over fitting on rare words

00:52:52.450 --> 00:52:54.113
very quickly.

00:52:54.113 --> 00:52:55.530
And so that's a
problem that could

00:52:55.530 --> 00:53:00.990
be addressed by instead using
a low rank representation

00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:03.480
of that Wsx matrix.

00:53:03.480 --> 00:53:07.950
In particular, you could think
about introducing a lower

00:53:07.950 --> 00:53:11.640
dimensional bottleneck
which, in this picture,

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:15.180
I'm noting as xt prime--

00:53:15.180 --> 00:53:18.480
which is your original
xt input, which

00:53:18.480 --> 00:53:21.960
is the one hot encoding,
multiplied by a new matrix We.

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:28.370
And then your recurrent
unit only takes

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:34.850
inputs of that xt
prime's dimension, which

00:53:34.850 --> 00:53:39.460
is k, which might be
dramatically smaller than v.

00:53:39.460 --> 00:53:41.290
And you can even think
about each column

00:53:41.290 --> 00:53:44.710
of that intermediate
representation We

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:46.480
as a word embedding.

00:53:49.240 --> 00:53:53.530
And this is something that Pete
talked to quite a bit about

00:53:53.530 --> 00:53:56.200
when we were talking my
natural language processing.

00:53:56.200 --> 00:53:59.230
And many of you would have
heard about in the context

00:53:59.230 --> 00:54:02.530
of things like word to vec.

00:54:02.530 --> 00:54:08.690
So if one wanted to take
a setting, for example,

00:54:08.690 --> 00:54:14.460
one institution's data where
you had a huge amount of data,

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:16.960
learn a recurrent neural network
on that institution's data,

00:54:16.960 --> 00:54:19.620
and then generalize it
to a new institution,

00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:22.060
one way of trying to do that--

00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:24.540
if you think about it, what
is the thing that you chop?

00:54:24.540 --> 00:54:27.300
One answer might be all you
do is keep the word embedding.

00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:30.180
So you might say, OK, I'm
going to keep the We's.

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:33.000
I'm going to translate
that to my new institution.

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:36.900
But I'm going to let the
recurrent parameters--

00:54:36.900 --> 00:54:40.090
for example, that
Wss, you might allow

00:54:40.090 --> 00:54:43.055
to be relearned for
each new institution.

00:54:43.055 --> 00:54:44.430
And so that might
be one approach

00:54:44.430 --> 00:54:46.710
of how to use the
same idea that we

00:54:46.710 --> 00:54:49.770
had from feed forward
neural networks

00:54:49.770 --> 00:54:53.700
within a recurrent setting.

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:57.110
Now all of this is very general.

00:54:57.110 --> 00:54:59.900
And what I want to do
next is to instantiate it

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:05.160
a bit in the context
of health care.

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:09.650
So since the time
that Pete presented

00:55:09.650 --> 00:55:15.220
the extensions of where to
vec, such as Bert and Elmo--

00:55:15.220 --> 00:55:17.480
and I'm not going
to go into them now,

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:20.270
but if you go back to Pete's
lecture from a few weeks

00:55:20.270 --> 00:55:22.660
ago to remind yourselves
what those were--

00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:24.410
since the time you
presented that lecture,

00:55:24.410 --> 00:55:25.970
there are actually
three new papers

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:30.080
that tried to apply this in
the health care context, one

00:55:30.080 --> 00:55:32.780
of which was from MIT.

00:55:32.780 --> 00:55:36.500
And so these papers all
have the same sort of idea.

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:39.780
They're going to
take some data set.

00:55:39.780 --> 00:55:43.590
And these papers all use Mimic.

00:55:43.590 --> 00:55:45.390
They're going to
take that text data.

00:55:45.390 --> 00:55:48.840
They're going to learn
some word embeddings

00:55:48.840 --> 00:55:50.520
or some low dimensional
representations

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:52.410
of all words in the vocabulary.

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:54.540
In this case,
they're not learning

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:56.310
a static representation
for each word.

00:55:56.310 --> 00:55:59.048
Instead, these Burt
and Elmo approaches

00:55:59.048 --> 00:56:00.840
are going to be learning
what you can think

00:56:00.840 --> 00:56:02.210
of as dynamic representations.

00:56:02.210 --> 00:56:03.960
They're going to be a
function of the word

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:07.470
and their context on the
left and right hand sides.

00:56:07.470 --> 00:56:09.810
What they'll do is then
take those representations

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:13.180
and attempt to use them
for a completely new task.

00:56:13.180 --> 00:56:17.230
Those new tasks might
be on Mimic data.

00:56:17.230 --> 00:56:20.430
So for example, these two tasks
are classification problems

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:21.330
on Mimic.

00:56:21.330 --> 00:56:23.260
But they might also
be on non Mimic data.

00:56:23.260 --> 00:56:27.540
So these two tasks are from
classification problems

00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:30.850
on clinical tasks that didn't
even come from Mimic at all.

00:56:30.850 --> 00:56:32.652
So it's really an
example of translating

00:56:32.652 --> 00:56:34.110
what you learn from
one institution

00:56:34.110 --> 00:56:35.670
to another institution.

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:37.440
These two data sets
were super small.

00:56:37.440 --> 00:56:40.530
Actually, all of these data
sets were really, really small

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.300
compared to the
original size of Mimic.

00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:44.925
So there might be some hope that
one could learn something that

00:56:44.925 --> 00:56:46.770
really improves generalization.

00:56:46.770 --> 00:56:48.940
And indeed, that's
what plays out.

00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:53.490
So all these tasks are looking
at a concept detection task.

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:59.260
Given a clinical note,
identify the segments of text

00:56:59.260 --> 00:57:01.270
within a note that
refer to, for example,

00:57:01.270 --> 00:57:04.690
a disorder or a treatment or
something else, which you then,

00:57:04.690 --> 00:57:08.235
in a second stage, might
normalize to the ULMS.

00:57:10.860 --> 00:57:14.070
So what's really striking
about these results

00:57:14.070 --> 00:57:18.570
is what happens when you go
from the left to the right

00:57:18.570 --> 00:57:20.250
column, which I'll
explain in a second,

00:57:20.250 --> 00:57:22.560
and what happens when
you go top to bottom

00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:24.880
across each one of
these different tasks.

00:57:24.880 --> 00:57:27.610
So the left column
are the results.

00:57:27.610 --> 00:57:32.400
And these results
are an f score.

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:36.240
The results, if you
were to use embeddings

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:39.790
trained on a
non-clinical data set--

00:57:39.790 --> 00:57:43.190
or definitely not on Mimic, but
on some other more general data

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:44.437
set.

00:57:44.437 --> 00:57:46.020
The second column
is what would happen

00:57:46.020 --> 00:57:49.720
if you trained those embeddings
on a clinical data set,

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:51.380
in this case Mimic.

00:57:51.380 --> 00:57:54.300
And you see pretty
big improvements

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:58.590
from the general embeddings
to the Mimic based embeddings.

00:57:58.590 --> 00:58:01.020
What's even more striking
is the improvements

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:04.330
that happen as you get
better and better embeddings.

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:07.050
So the first row are
the results if you

00:58:07.050 --> 00:58:09.480
were to use just word
to vec embeddings.

00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:15.570
And so for example, for
the ITB2 challenge in 2010,

00:58:15.570 --> 00:58:20.730
you get 82.65 f score using
word to vec embeddings.

00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:23.310
And if you use a very
large Burt embedding,

00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:28.880
you get 90.25 f
score, f measure,

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:31.870
which is substantially higher.

00:58:31.870 --> 00:58:34.190
And the same findings were
found time and time again

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:36.920
across different tasks.

00:58:36.920 --> 00:58:39.790
Now what I find really
striking about these results

00:58:39.790 --> 00:58:43.180
is that I had tried many of
these things a couple of years

00:58:43.180 --> 00:58:45.930
ago, not using Bert
or Elmo but using word

00:58:45.930 --> 00:58:48.390
to vec, glove, and fast text.

00:58:48.390 --> 00:58:52.060
And what I found is that
using word embedding

00:58:52.060 --> 00:58:54.610
approaches for these problems--

00:58:54.610 --> 00:58:57.430
even if you threw that in as
additional features on top

00:58:57.430 --> 00:59:01.510
of other state of
the art approaches

00:59:01.510 --> 00:59:04.210
to this concept
extraction problem--

00:59:04.210 --> 00:59:06.580
did not improve
predictive performance

00:59:06.580 --> 00:59:09.100
above the existing
state of the art.

00:59:09.100 --> 00:59:12.340
However in this paper,
here they used the simplest

00:59:12.340 --> 00:59:13.760
possible algorithm.

00:59:13.760 --> 00:59:15.700
They used a recurrent
neural network

00:59:15.700 --> 00:59:18.130
fed into a conditional
random field

00:59:18.130 --> 00:59:21.670
for the purpose of classifying
each word into each

00:59:21.670 --> 00:59:23.420
of these categories.

00:59:23.420 --> 00:59:27.160
And the features that they
used are just these embedding

00:59:27.160 --> 00:59:28.270
features.

00:59:28.270 --> 00:59:30.360
So with just the word to
vec embedding features,

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:31.360
the performance is crap.

00:59:31.360 --> 00:59:33.760
You don't get anywhere
close to the state of art.

00:59:33.760 --> 00:59:37.720
But with the better
embeddings, they actually

00:59:37.720 --> 00:59:40.480
improved on the state of
the art for every single one

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:43.590
of these tasks.

00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:47.240
And that is without any of the
manual feature engineering.